{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`scripts/mutation_gate.py` isolates the **source tree** (`git archive` into a temp dir)\nbut not **runtime effects**. On 2026-08-26 a mutation removed a guard from a shell\nscript that performs real deletion, a test exec'd it with an empty path variable, and\nthe glob expanded to `rm -rf /*` — destroying `/opt/homebrew` and every Homebrew app in\n`/Applications` on a contributor workstation. Only a 60-second subprocess timeout\nstopped it, with `/Users` still ahead of it in the target list.\n\nNo mutation tool helps: cargo-mutants, mutmut, StrykerJS, PIT and mull isolate the\nsource tree, but **none isolates runtime effects** — a copied tree does nothing when\nmutated code calls `rm -rf /` (cargo-mutants documents this hazard:\nhttps://mutants.rs/cautions.html). And because the harness string-substitutes the\nartifact under test, any guard **inside** it is mutable — only an outer layer holds.\n\nScope is the **OS sandbox only**; the static lint gate here was split to\n`6818-615f-555e-4bb9` (G5).\n\n## Which subprocess matters\n\nOf the four subprocesses `execute_shard()` spawns via `_run()`, only two execute shard\ntest code: the baseline `pytest` (579) and `mutmut run` (583); `mutmut results` (595)\nand `show` (612) are reporting-only. The incident ran\nunder `mutmut run`, which executes the *mutated* code, so sandboxing only the baseline\nwould leave the causal path exposed. A THIRD mutant-executing site sits outside this\nfunction — `_diagnose_non_killed` (660) re-runs surviving mutants unsandboxed — out of\nscope, tracked as `724c-b5fd-efb1-40d5`.\n\n## Mechanisms\n\n**macOS — Seatbelt.** `sandbox-exec` is Apple-deprecated, so its enforcement is a\nload-bearing external capability. Measured on this host (macOS 26.5.2) with\n`(allow default)` + `(deny file-write*)` + an allow-list subpath, the three probes were:\nwrite outside DENIED and the file absent; write inside succeeded; `rm -rf <protected>/*`\n(the incident's shape) DENIED with the target surviving. Deprecation is a future risk,\nnot a current gap — the fail-closed posture turns a breakage into an abort.\n\n**Linux — bubblewrap only.** `unshare --map-root-user --mount` is **dropped**: it does\nnot deny writes without a read-only remount, and unprivileged `CLONE_NEWUSER` is\ndisabled on many hardened hosts, so it can probe present yet fail to enforce — worse\nthan no sandbox, because it looks safe. Without `bwrap`, Linux fails closed.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Both shard-test-executing subprocesses in `execute_shard()` — the baseline\n      `pytest` (579) **and** the `mutmut run` mutant test-run (583) — are wrapped in an\n      OS-enforced sandbox denying writes outside an explicit allow-list. The venv is\n      deliberately EXCLUDED from it, so a mutant cannot drop executable code into\n      site-packages for a later un-sandboxed phase to import. Verified by\n      `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`.\n- [x] The enforcement test trips the deny under **`mutmut run`** specifically, not only\n      under the baseline `pytest`, since `mutmut run` is the path the incident took.\n- [x] The sandbox demonstrably DENIES: a probe writing outside the allow-list exits\n      non-zero and the file is absent afterward; the same probe inside the allow-list\n      succeeds. Asserting wrapper argv alone does not satisfy this — the test observes\n      enforcement, not construction.\n- [x] macOS uses Seatbelt `sandbox-exec` with a generated profile, reproducing the\n      three probes measured above. Verified by `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py`\n      on a macOS host.\n- [x] Linux uses `bwrap`, probed at runtime. No `unshare` fallback. Verified by a test\n      asserting the probe selects `bwrap` when present and returns \"unavailable\" when\n      absent.\n- [x] The Linux write-deny is proven **on Linux**, not inferred from macOS: the three\n      probes were measured by hand in a `ubuntu:24.04` container (outside DENIED, inside\n      succeeded, `rm -rf <protected>/*` DENIED with the target surviving). It is NOT\n      proven by a green `ubuntu-latest` leg — those runners deny unprivileged user\n      namespaces (see below), so the tests SKIP and a green leg proves nothing. That\n      gating gap is tracked as `0d22-f664-77e1-495f`.\n- [x] The sandbox wrapper preserves the existing `mutmut run --max-children 1`\n      concurrency contract asserted at `tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py:948`.\n- [x] With no sandbox mechanism available the run ABORTS non-zero with a message naming\n      `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED`. Verified by a test stubbing the mechanism\n      absent and asserting that literal appears in stderr.\n- [x] `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED=1` waives the sandbox and logs at WARNING; a\n      silent unsandboxed fallback is rejected. Verified by asserting the WARNING record\n      names the same literal.\n- [x] Enforcement is an OS facility invoked in-process, never a CI-provider feature, and\n      works from a developer laptop with no CI involved (`project.portability`).\n- [x] `HOME` is set to a non-existent path for both sandboxed subprocesses so adjacent\n      home-directory writes fail loudly. Verified by RUNNING a child and asserting the\n      `HOME` it observes (unsandboxed, and through `wrap()`), plus a binding test tying\n      both wrapped `_run` calls to the hardened env — each proven RED by real mutation.\n      Asserting only the mapping `sandbox_env` returns is insufficient: with that alone,\n      deleting the call left all 70 tests green.\n- [x] `probe()` verifies `bwrap` FUNCTIONALLY, not by `shutil.which`. Presence is not\n      capability: this repo's runners ship bubblewrap but deny unprivileged user\n      namespaces (`setting up uid map: Permission denied`), so a PATH-only probe reports\n      available then fails to enforce — the flaw that disqualified `unshare`. Verified by\n      `test_bwrap_present_but_unable_to_namespace_is_not_offered`.\n- [x] On a CI runner (`CI` truthy) with no mechanism the gate proceeds UNSANDBOXED with\n      a WARNING; on a workstation it still ABORTS — a runner is already the disposable\n      environment the sandbox substitutes for. Keyed on the cross-provider `CI`\n      convention, naming no provider (`project.portability`). Verified by\n      `test_ci_runner_proceeds_unsandboxed_with_a_warning` and\n      `test_workstation_still_aborts_when_no_sandbox`.\n- [x] A usable sandbox is used even on CI — `CI` is a fallback for the ABSENCE of a\n      mechanism, never a licence to skip a working one. Verified by\n      `test_a_working_sandbox_is_used_even_on_ci`.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_sandbox.py` — enforcement under the baseline and\n  `mutmut run`, probing, fail-closed abort, opt-out WARNING, and `HOME`.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/test_mutation_gate.py` — still green, including the\n  `--max-children 1` concurrency contract at line 948 the wrapper must preserve.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `check_complexity_baseline.py --check`.\n\n## Definition of Done\n\n- Every acceptance criterion above is checked by the command it names.\n- The sandbox logic lands in its own module: `mutation_gate.py` is **762 LOC against the\n  800 hard cap**, so it cannot absorb this inline.\n- An operator note documents `REBAR_MUTATION_ALLOW_UNSANDBOXED` and its risk.\n\n## Platform scope\n\nPOSIX only: `pyproject.toml:37-38` classifies it `Operating System :: POSIX` and\n`MacOS :: MacOS X`, and the CI matrix is `[ubuntu-latest, macos-latest]`. Windows is\nout of scope.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled from the 2026-08-26 incident; two unrelated leaks found that day are tracked as\n`a37c-d55c-72c3-439b` and `f3d9-ecd8-df72-4fa1`. Plan review caught six real defects,\nall corrected above: `mutmut run` scoping (G6), the unnamed opt-out variable (E2), the\nunverified `unshare` write-deny claim (T1/T3), AC6's false claim that a green\n`ubuntu-latest` leg proves Linux enforcement, AC1's venv contradiction, and AC11's\nunverified `HOME` claim. Four follow-ups are linked `discovered_from` this ticket."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787859559315316001,"uuid":"a164af1d-0830-4bdd-8b1f-96dea1ac3d80"}